      title: "Moses and the Burning Bush",
      body: `## A new king

The book of **Exodus** (Greek, "going out") begins where Genesis ends, with the descendants of Jacob in Egypt. They have multiplied, and "the land was filled with them." Then:

> "Now there arose up a new king over Egypt, which knew not Joseph." (Exodus 1:8, King James Version)

The new Pharaoh fears the Israelites, and enslaves them, setting taskmasters over them to build the store cities of **Pithom** and **Raamses**. When they continue to multiply, he orders the Hebrew midwives, **Shiphrah** and **Puah**, to kill every boy born to the Hebrew women. The midwives, "fearing God," refuse, and tell Pharaoh that the Hebrew women give birth before the midwives arrive (1:15–21). Their quiet act of **civil disobedience** is the first act of resistance in the story, and it is carried out by women. Pharaoh then commands all his people to throw every Hebrew boy into the Nile.

## The child in the basket

A woman of the tribe of Levi bears a son, hides him for three months, and then places him in a basket of bulrushes, coated with pitch, among the reeds of the river. The word for "basket," *tevah*, is the same as the word for Noah's "ark." Pharaoh's daughter finds the child, has compassion on him, and, through the quick thinking of the child's sister, who has been watching, hires his own mother to nurse him. She names him **Moses**, "because I drew him out of the water" (2:10). The name is probably Egyptian, related to the element *-mose*, "son of," or "born," found in names like Thutmose and Ahmose; the Hebrew text connects it with the verb *mashah*, "to draw out." A similar story of a baby set adrift in a basket was told of **Sargon of Akkad**, the Mesopotamian king of the twenty-fourth century BCE.

## Moses in Midian

Moses grows up in Pharaoh's household. One day, he sees an Egyptian beating a Hebrew, "one of his brethren," and, looking this way and that, kills the Egyptian and hides him in the sand. When he learns that the deed is known, he flees to **Midian**, in the desert east of the Gulf of Aqaba (2:11–15). There, at a well, he defends the seven daughters of the priest **Jethro** (also called Reuel) against shepherds, and marries one of them, **Zipporah**, in the betrothal type-scene you met in lesson 1. He names his son **Gershom**, "for he said, I have been a stranger in a strange land" (2:22).

Meanwhile, in Egypt, the Israelites groan under their bondage, "and God heard their groaning, and God remembered his covenant with Abraham, with Isaac, and with Jacob" (2:24).

## The burning bush

Moses is tending Jethro's flock at **Horeb**, "the mountain of God," when he sees a strange sight:

> "And the angel of the LORD appeared unto him in a flame of fire out of the midst of a bush: and he looked, and, behold, the bush burned with fire, and the bush was not consumed. And Moses said, I will now turn aside, and see this great sight, why the bush is not burnt." (Exodus 3:2–3)

God calls to him from the bush: "Moses, Moses." "Here am I." "Draw not nigh hither: put off thy shoes from off thy feet, for the place whereon thou standest is holy ground" (3:4–5). God reveals himself as the God of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, and says that he has seen the affliction of his people and heard their cry, and has come down to deliver them, and bring them to "a land flowing with milk and honey." And he will send **Moses** to Pharaoh (3:7–10).

## The reluctant prophet

Moses objects, five times. "Who am I, that I should go unto Pharaoh?" God answers: "Certainly I will be with thee" (3:11–12). What if the people ask your name? (3:13). What if they do not believe me? God gives him signs: his staff becomes a serpent, his hand becomes leprous and is healed (4:1–9). "O my Lord, I am not eloquent, … but I am slow of speech, and of a slow tongue." God answers: "Who hath made man's mouth? … have not I the LORD?" (4:10–11). And finally, simply: "O my Lord, send, I pray thee, by the hand of him whom thou wilt send," that is, send someone else. God, angry, appoints Moses's brother **Aaron** to speak for him (4:13–16).

The reluctance of Moses is a pattern repeated with later prophets, such as Jeremiah, who protests that he is only a child (Jeremiah 1:6). The great leaders of Israel are not those who seek power, but those who are called against their will.

## The divine name

The most important moment in the scene is Moses's second question:

> "Behold, when I come unto the children of Israel, and shall say unto them, The God of your fathers hath sent me unto you; and they shall say to me, What is his name? what shall I say unto them? And God said unto Moses, **I AM THAT I AM**: and he said, Thus shalt thou say unto the children of Israel, I AM hath sent me unto you." (Exodus 3:13–14)

The Hebrew phrase is ***ehyeh asher ehyeh***. It is formed from the verb *hayah*, "to be," and can be translated "I am who I am," "I will be what I will be," or "I am the one who is." God then gives the name by which he will be known: **YHWH**, which appears to be related to the same verb (3:15). This is the **divine name**, the "Tetragrammaton" (Greek, "four letters"), which appears more than six thousand times in the Hebrew Bible.

Its original pronunciation is uncertain, because, as you saw in lesson 1, Jews ceased to pronounce it, out of reverence, sometime in the Second Temple period, and read *Adonai*, "my Lord," instead. Many scholars think it was pronounced **Yahweh**. The form "**Jehovah**," used in some English Bibles, arose in the late Middle Ages from combining the consonants YHWH with the vowels of *Adonai*. In the King James Version and most English translations, it is rendered "the LORD" in small capitals.

What does the name mean? The answer at the bush seems both to reveal and to conceal. Some interpreters take it as a statement of God's eternal, self-sufficient **being**: the Septuagint translated it *egō eimi ho ōn*, "I am the one who is," and Christian theologians, from Augustine to Aquinas, read it as a philosophical definition of God as pure Being. Others take it as a promise of God's **presence** in history: "I will be with you, as I will be," a God who will be known by what he does. Others think it is a refusal to give a name that could be used to control God: "I am who I am; that is all you need to know."

> **Pause and consider.** Moses asks God's name, and is told, "I AM THAT I AM." Is this an answer, or a refusal to answer? What might it mean for a God to be known by a name like this?`,
