      title: "The Plagues, the Passover, and the Sea",
      body: `## "Let my people go"

Moses and Aaron go to Pharaoh, and say: "Thus saith the LORD God of Israel, **Let my people go**, that they may hold a feast unto me in the wilderness" (Exodus 5:1). Pharaoh answers: "Who is the LORD, that I should obey his voice to let Israel go? I know not the LORD, neither will I let Israel go" (5:2). Instead, he makes the Israelites' labor harder, forcing them to gather their own straw for bricks while producing the same number. The Israelites turn against Moses, and Moses complains to God: "Wherefore hast thou so evil entreated this people?" (5:22).

The phrase "Let my people go" became, centuries later, the refrain of the African American spiritual "Go Down, Moses," and the Exodus story became the central story of enslaved African Americans, who saw in it the promise of their own deliverance. Harriet Tubman, who led many enslaved people to freedom, was called "Moses." The story has inspired liberation movements around the world, from Latin American liberation theology to the civil rights movement. Martin Luther King Jr., on the night before his death in 1968, said that he had "been to the mountaintop" and seen "the promised land," echoing the death of Moses on Mount Nebo.

## The plagues

God sends **ten plagues** on Egypt (Exodus 7–12): the Nile turned to **blood**; **frogs**; **lice** (or gnats); **flies**; a pestilence on the **livestock**; **boils**; **hail**; **locusts**; **darkness**; and, finally, the death of the **firstborn**. The plagues grow in severity, and they are presented as a contest between YHWH and the gods of Egypt: "against all the gods of Egypt I will execute judgment: I am the LORD" (12:12). The Nile, the frogs, the cattle, and the sun were all associated with Egyptian deities. Pharaoh's magicians match the first two plagues, but then fail, and admit: "This is the finger of God" (8:19).

After each plague, Pharaoh relents, and then changes his mind. The text says, at different points, that Pharaoh **hardened** his heart (8:15), and that **God hardened** Pharaoh's heart (9:12; 10:1). This has raised a serious theological problem: if God hardened Pharaoh's heart, how can Pharaoh be held responsible? Interpreters have suggested that God confirmed a choice Pharaoh had already made; that God hardened his heart only after he had hardened it himself several times; or that the text holds human freedom and divine sovereignty together without resolving their relationship. Paul discusses the question in Romans 9:17–18.

Some modern writers have tried to explain the plagues as natural events, such as a chain of ecological disasters following an algal bloom in the Nile, or the effects of a volcanic eruption on the island of Thera (Santorin), which occurred around 1600 BCE. Such explanations are speculative, and miss the point of the story, which presents the plagues as acts of God revealing his power over nature and over Pharaoh.

## The Passover

The tenth plague is the death of the firstborn of Egypt, "from the firstborn of Pharaoh that sitteth upon his throne, even unto the firstborn of the maidservant that is behind the mill" (11:5). Before it, God gives Moses instructions for the **Passover** (Exodus 12). Each Israelite household is to kill a **lamb** without blemish, put its blood on the doorposts and lintel of the house, and eat it roasted, with **unleavened bread** and **bitter herbs**, in haste, "with your loins girded, your shoes on your feet, and your staff in your hand" (12:11). At midnight, the LORD passes through Egypt and strikes the firstborn, but **passes over** the houses marked with blood. "And there was a great cry in Egypt; for there was not a house where there was not one dead" (12:30).

The Passover is the most important festival of the Jewish year. It is commanded as a **memorial** for all generations: "And thou shalt shew thy son in that day, saying, This is done because of that which the LORD did unto me when I came forth out of Egypt" (13:8). At the Passover meal, the **Seder**, Jewish families retell the story of the Exodus, using a text called the **Haggadah** ("telling"), which includes the instruction: "In every generation, each person must see himself as if he had personally come out of Egypt" (Mishnah, *Pesachim* 10:5). The past is not merely remembered; it is relived.

For Christians, the Passover is connected with the death of **Jesus**, which, according to the Gospels, took place at Passover time. The Last Supper is presented in the first three Gospels as a Passover meal, and Paul wrote: "Christ our passover is sacrificed for us" (1 Corinthians 5:7).

## The sea

Pharaoh at last lets the people go, and they leave Egypt, some six hundred thousand men on foot, besides women and children, and "a mixed multitude" (12:37–38). God leads them by a pillar of cloud by day and a pillar of fire by night. Then Pharaoh changes his mind once more, and pursues them with his chariots, and catches up with them by the sea. The people panic, but Moses says: "Fear ye not, stand still, and see the salvation of the LORD" (14:13). Moses stretches out his hand, a strong east wind blows all night, and the waters are divided; the Israelites go through "upon the dry ground: and the waters were a wall unto them on their right hand, and on their left." When the Egyptians follow, the waters return, and cover them (14:21–28).

The sea is traditionally called the **Red Sea**, from the Septuagint. But the Hebrew is ***yam suf***, which most scholars translate "**Sea of Reeds**," perhaps a marshy lake in the eastern Nile delta. Its location is unknown.

Then Moses and the Israelites sing a song of triumph, the **Song of the Sea** (Exodus 15), one of the oldest poems in the Bible: "I will sing unto the LORD, for he hath triumphed gloriously: the horse and his rider hath he thrown into the sea" (15:1). And **Miriam**, the prophetess, Moses's sister, takes a timbrel, and all the women follow her with timbrels and dances, singing the same refrain (15:20–21).

## Did the Exodus happen?

There is no Egyptian record of the Exodus, of Moses, or of Hebrew slaves, and the figure of six hundred thousand men, implying a population of two million or more, is impossible for the period. Many historians think that the story, as we have it, is not history in the modern sense. But many also think it preserves a memory of some **historical** experience: perhaps a small group of Semitic slaves who escaped from Egypt, and whose story became the founding story of a larger people. Egyptian records do show that Semitic people lived and worked in Egypt, that some were slaves, and that the city of Pi-Ramesses was built in the eastern delta under **Ramesses II** (ruled about 1279–1213 BCE), who is often identified, without certainty, as the Pharaoh of the Exodus. Whatever its historical basis, the Exodus became the central story of Israel's identity: the God of Israel is "the LORD thy God, which have brought thee out of the land of Egypt, out of the house of bondage" (20:2).

> **Pause and consider.** The Passover Haggadah says that "in every generation, each person must see himself as if he had personally come out of Egypt." What does it mean to remember a story as if one had been there? Why might a community tell its founding story this way?`,
