      body: `## The conquest

The book of **Joshua**, the first of the Former Prophets, tells how **Joshua**, Moses's successor, led the Israelites across the **Jordan** into Canaan, on dry ground, as at the Red Sea (Joshua 3–4), and conquered the land. The first city is **Jericho**, whose walls fall down after the Israelites march around it for seven days, and on the seventh day blow trumpets and shout (Joshua 6). A Canaanite prostitute, **Rahab**, who hid the Israelite spies and declared her faith in Israel's God, is spared with her family (Joshua 2; 6:22–25); the Gospel of Matthew lists her as an ancestor of Jesus (Matthew 1:5). The book describes a swift and total conquest in a series of campaigns, and then the division of the land among the twelve tribes (Joshua 13–21). It ends with Joshua's farewell speech and the renewal of the covenant at Shechem, where Joshua challenges the people: "choose you this day whom ye will serve; … but as for me and my house, we will serve the LORD" (24:15).

## The ban

The book of Joshua contains one of the most troubling themes in the Bible: the ***herem***, the "**ban**," by which the inhabitants of conquered cities were "devoted" to God by being destroyed. At Jericho, "they utterly destroyed all that was in the city, both man and woman, young and old, and ox, and sheep, and ass, with the edge of the sword" (6:21). Deuteronomy commands that the peoples of Canaan be utterly destroyed, "thou shalt save alive nothing that breatheth" (Deuteronomy 20:16), so that they will not teach Israel to worship their gods.

How should readers understand these texts? The practice of the ban was not unique to Israel: the **Mesha Stele**, which you read about in lesson 1, records that Mesha, king of Moab, captured the Israelite town of Nebo and killed its seven thousand inhabitants, "for I had devoted them to destruction (*hrm*) for Ashtar-Chemosh." Many scholars doubt that the conquest happened as Joshua describes, since archaeology does not support a sudden destruction of Canaanite cities around 1200 BCE, and the book of Judges itself presents a very different picture, of a slow and incomplete settlement in which Canaanites continued to live among the Israelites. Some think the conquest narratives were written centuries later, in the age of Josiah, as an idealized and ideological account. Jewish and Christian interpreters have struggled with these texts for centuries: some have read them allegorically, as the conquest of sin in the soul; others as a unique command for a unique time, not to be repeated; others as a record of human violence attributed to God, which other parts of the Bible, such as the prophets' visions of peace, implicitly criticize. They remain a profound moral challenge, and they have, tragically, been cited to justify the conquest and destruction of other peoples, including Native Americans.

## Judges

The book of **Judges** tells a darker and more realistic story. After Joshua's death, the Israelites fail to drive out the Canaanites, and live among them. The book is built on a repeated **cycle** (Judges 2:11–19):

1. The Israelites "did evil in the sight of the LORD," and worshiped the Baals.
2. God gave them into the hands of their enemies, who oppressed them.
3. They cried to God.
4. God raised up a **judge** (*shofet*), a charismatic military leader, who delivered them.
5. The land had rest, until the judge died, and the cycle began again.

The judges are not judges in the modern sense, but **heroes** and **deliverers**, and they are a strange collection. **Ehud**, a left-handed man, assassinates the fat Moabite king Eglon in his private chamber with a hidden sword (Judges 3). **Deborah**, a prophetess who "judged Israel" sitting under a palm tree, summons the general **Barak** to battle against the Canaanites; and **Jael**, a woman of the Kenite tribe, kills the fleeing Canaanite general **Sisera** by driving a tent peg through his head as he sleeps in her tent (Judges 4). The **Song of Deborah** (Judges 5), which celebrates the victory, is one of the oldest poems in the Bible. **Gideon** defeats the Midianites with three hundred men carrying trumpets and torches in jars (Judges 7). **Jephthah** makes a rash vow to sacrifice whatever comes out of his house to meet him if he returns victorious, and his only daughter comes out dancing (Judges 11). **Samson**, the strong man, is undone by his weakness for women, above all **Delilah**, who discovers that his strength lies in his uncut hair, and betrays him to the Philistines; blinded and imprisoned, he pulls down the temple of the Philistine god Dagon upon himself and three thousand Philistines (Judges 13–16).

## No king in Israel

As the book goes on, the judges become more flawed, and the cycle spirals downward. The last chapters (17–21) describe idolatry, the slaughter of a peaceful town, the rape and murder of a Levite's concubine, and a civil war in which the tribe of Benjamin is almost wiped out. The book repeats a refrain:

> "In those days there was no king in Israel: every man did that which was right in his own eyes." (Judges 21:25)

The refrain points forward to the **monarchy**, which will be the subject of lesson 4. Judges seems to argue that Israel needed a king to save it from anarchy. But the books that follow will show that kings bring problems of their own.

## Ruth: a different story from the same age

The book of **Ruth**, which in the Christian Bible follows Judges, is set "in the days when the judges ruled" (Ruth 1:1), yet it could hardly be more different. It tells of a **Moabite** widow, a foreigner from a people Deuteronomy excluded from the assembly of the LORD (Deuteronomy 23:3), who refuses to abandon her Israelite mother-in-law Naomi: "whither thou goest, I will go; … thy people shall be my people, and thy God my God" (Ruth 1:16). Through her loyalty (*hesed*), she becomes the great-grandmother of King David. Placed beside the violence of Joshua and Judges, Ruth offers another vision of how outsiders may join Israel: not by conquest, but by faithfulness. We will return to it in lesson 5, where it belongs in the Jewish order, among the Writings.

> **Pause and consider.** The book of Joshua describes the destruction of whole cities as a command of God, while Judges shows Israel living among the Canaanites. How should a modern reader approach texts of violence in a sacred book? What resources does the Bible itself offer for criticizing them?`,
